.TH LOGIN 1
.SH NAME
login  \-  sign on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B login
[
.I username
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I login
command
is used when a user initially
signs on.
It is normally reached by
.IR getty (8),
which reads the name and passes it as the argument above.
.PP
If
.I login
is invoked without an argument,
it asks for a user name, and, if
appropriate, a password.
Echoing is turned off during the typing of the password,
so it will not appear on the written record of the
session.
A name that is not in the password file is still asked for a password
and still refused: this is deliberate, since a
.I login
that skipped the prompt would say which names exist.
.PP
After a successful login,
accounting files are updated and
the user is informed of the
existence of
mail and message-of-the-day files.
.I Login
initializes the user and group IDs and the working directory,
then executes a command interpreter (usually
.IR  sh (1))
according to specifications found in a password file.
Argument 0 of the command interpreter is `\-sh'.
.SH "THIS MACHINE"
.B Note:
three of the files below are not on this system, and each fails
quietly rather than being cut out of the program:
.TP
/usr/adm/wtmp
The permanent login history.
Nothing reads it \- there is no
.I last
here \- so it is not on the image and the append is skipped.
.TP
/usr/spool/mail
There is no
.IR mail (1),
so `You have mail.' never prints.
.TP
/usr/bin
Named by the PATH
.I login
exports, and empty of meaning: the shell's own default already
reaches /bin.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 says
.I login
`may be used at any time to change from one user to another'.
Not here.
That use needs the program to be set-user-ID root, and it is not \-
.IR getty (8)
is already root and hands the privilege on.
The command that changes user is
.IR su (1),
which is not yet ported.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 says
.I login
`is recognized by
.IR sh (1)
and executed directly (without forking)'.
That was the pre-Bourne shell.
.IR Sh (1)
here has no such built-in and forks like any other command.
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/usr/spool/mail/*\ \ 'u
/etc/utmp	accounting
.br
/usr/adm/wtmp	accounting; absent here
.br
/usr/spool/mail/*	mail; absent here
.br
/etc/motd	message-of-the-day
.br
/etc/passwd	password file
.SH "SEE ALSO"
init(8), newgrp(1), getty(8), mail(1), passwd(1), passwd(5), ttys(5)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
`Login incorrect,'
if the name or the password is bad.
.br
`No directory', if the account's home directory cannot be reached;
`No shell', if the command interpreter cannot be executed.
